# Store-to-load forwarding test for ooo_cpu_ref (stage 3c). 16-bit hex words,
# one per address from 0. Encoding: [15:13] opcode [12:10] rd [9:7] ra [6:4] rb [7:0] imm
# A load that misses in the D-cache (address 0 is only in the I-cache) feeds an
# ADD; behind them a store with address and data ready cannot perform (it is
# not the head) while the ADD is in flight, and a load to the same address
# FORWARDS the store's data as soon as the missing load has left the memory
# slot (mem_fwd is asserted at that ld_perform). The single memory slot is
# oldest-first, so forwarding only helps when the store is stuck behind older
# non-memory work -- which is exactly this shape.
# Expected: r3 = 0x30, r4 = 7, r5 = 0x6c30 (word at address 0), r1 = 0xd860, r6 = 7.
6c30        # 0: LI   r3, 0x30
7007        # 1: LI   r4, 7
9400        # 2: LD   r5, [r0]          -> 0x6c30, a D-cache miss (slow)
26d0        # 3: ADD  r1, r5, r5        -> waits for r5; keeps the store from the head
a1c0        # 4: ST   [r3], r4          -> ready, but not head
9980        # 5: LD   r6, [r3]          -> forwards 7 from the store above
0000        # 6: NOP
0000
0000
0000
